ility  um and over the long term what is that  is going to inevitably do  is increase attrition rates amongst  women  more than men  so in terms of recommendations based on  that data we would suggest that the work  practices that have been put in place  post uh during the pandemic should be  continued post pandemic  but be aware that the youngest and  oldest in the industry may  prefer more traditional patterns of  working or may need more support  adapting to post-pandemic norms  introduce or expand flexible working  policies the main allow staff to  maintain a work-life balance and reduce  attrition rates of women as tenure  increases  so that's the data um i have kind of  gone through all the recommendations but  i think it's worth  just going through these again  um  unless we measure and track something we  can't improve it and we want to improve  the balance  of women  in senior leadership roles so measure  and track that gender balance  and if you don't like the figures do  something about it  measure and track gender balance in  training  if you don't like those figures do  something about it if you are a manager  um who has responsibility for staff  and have a look at your own decision  making how many women are you putting  through for training compared to the men  in your teams  is that  proportion the same  and if it's not it should be  focus on progression forming towards  senior leadership roles during  mid-career there is a real danger point  for women in the middle of their careers  um and that has an effect later on  create gender balance in leadership  development pipelines  so that we have a  set of women who are being trained and  given the opportunities to flex  um  their managerial muscles so that they  are ready to enter into those leadership  pipelines and take those senior roles  and give access to women to those male  networks of influence  initiate unconscious bias trainings and  opportunities for women to tell their  stories in order to 